{
  "suite": "jwt",
  "title": "RS256 JWT verification against host-supplied keys",
  "contract": {
    "summary": "`verifyJwt` (TS) / `Jwt::verify` (PHP): verify a JWS compact token against keys the HOST supplies (the core never fetches a key set). RS256 only; alg none and HS256 refused by name; a kid must name a supplied key; exp REQUIRED; nbf honoured; audience REQUIRED unless the caller passes null on purpose; issuer matched when given. Leeway 60 s. On success the claims are returned verbatim.",
    "reference": "authored",
    "referenceNote": "0001 is RFC 7515 Appendix A.2, the published RS256 example, which verifies from the RFC's own JWK and token (checked 2026-11-01). The other rows are signed by a throwaway 2048-bit key generated when this table was written; only its PUBLIC JWK is stored. Reasons are fixed strings. 0015-0024 (added 2026-11-06, Fancy's review) are signed by a further throwaway key k3, its public JWK in each row: wrong signature lengths, values at and above the modulus, and VALID raw-RSA signatures over malformed EMSA-PKCS1-v1_5 blocks whose last 32 bytes are still the right digest, so only a byte-for-byte comparison of the whole block refuses all of them. 0015 is their control."
  },
